INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Where the Founder window stands going into September.', 'Email', '2026-09-03', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'A month in, the Founder cohort is taking shape.

The math one more time: $99 a month plus $49 per additional location, locked 24 months. Setup $250 plus $50. After the 250 fill, $199 plus $99. No date deadline — the window closes at seat 250 and not before.

If you''ve taken a tour, you already know what EvidLY shows you. If you haven''t, the seats that fit your kitchen best tend to get claimed first.

The first 250 kitchen leaders aren''t just customers to me. They''re the ones betting on something new because they believe the visibility gap shouldn''t exist, and because somebody has to go first.

I''m a USAF veteran. Opening this window on the Fourth of July wasn''t a marketing gimmick — 250 seats for 250 years of this country. I won''t forget who showed up first.

Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.

Arthur Haggerty
Founder, EvidLY
founders@getevidly.com · (855) 384-3591', 'CLAIM YOUR SEAT', 'Email', '[copy_state: Final] [source: EvidLY-Email]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'EvidLY' AND c.channel_label = 'Email' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-09-03' AND c.title = 'Where the Founder window stands going into September.');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Prove: the part nobody thinks about until they need it.', 'Email', '2026-09-07', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'We''ve spent this month on Predict and Reduce. This is the third one, and it''s the one that gets ignored until the day it''s the only thing that matters.

Prove.

Every kitchen leader I''ve met can describe how they run their kitchen. Very few can demonstrate it — on demand, to someone skeptical, from records, without a week of preparation.

Those are different capabilities. The first one is competence. The second one is what an inspector, a carrier, or a buyer actually evaluates.

The gap between them is where good operations get hurt. A kitchen that did the work but can''t produce the evidence occupies the same position as one that didn''t. That''s harsh and it''s the actual standard.

Prove isn''t paperwork for its own sake. It''s the mechanism that converts everything you already do into something that holds up when someone asks.

Here''s the test worth running today. Without preparation, from your phone, in under ten minutes: twelve months of hood cleaning documentation with scope, your suppression service history, your last three inspection reports, current training certifications, and dated photographs of system condition.

Most operations can''t. That''s not a character flaw — it''s a structural gap almost nobody addressed, because addressing it while nothing is wrong requires foresight nobody rewards.

Until they do.

Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.

Arthur Haggerty
Founder, EvidLY
founders@getevidly.com · (855) 384-3591', 'SEE YOUR PROOF CHAIN', 'Email', '[copy_state: Final] [source: EvidLY-Email]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'EvidLY' AND c.channel_label = 'Email' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-09-07' AND c.title = 'Prove: the part nobody thinks about until they need it.');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Predict. Reduce. Prove. Why those three, in that order.', 'Email', '2026-09-10', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'A month of this and it''s worth saying plainly why the framework is what it is.

**Predict.** Equipment drifts. Enforcement follows patterns. Risk accumulates in ways that are legible in advance to anyone positioned to see them. Identifying that early is the difference between choosing your timing and reacting to someone else''s.

**Reduce.** Not prevent. I''m deliberate about that word. You cannot prevent every lapse in a commercial kitchen — anyone selling you prevention is selling you something that doesn''t exist. What you can do is reduce probability and consequence, systematically, with the time that prediction bought you.

**Prove.** Everything above is worthless in the moment that matters unless you can demonstrate it. Prove is what converts good operations into defensible ones.

The order matters. Prediction without reduction is anxiety. Reduction without proof is invisible — you did the work and can''t show it. Proof without the first two is theater.

All three, in sequence, is a kitchen that knows what''s coming, acted on it, and can show its work.

That''s the entire product. Everything else is implementation detail.

The Founder window is still open — 250 seats, 24-month lock, no date. It closes at seat 250.

Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.

Arthur Haggerty
Founder, EvidLY
