INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Letter grade, number, or nothing: how your county actually evaluates your kitchen', 'Blog', '2026-08-11', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'Two identical kitchens. Same menu, same equipment, same standards, same violations found on the same day. One walks away with an A in the window. One walks away with a 91 posted online. One walks away with nothing published at all.

The difference isn''t the kitchen. It''s the county.

**There is no "the health department"**

Across the five states EvidLY covers there are 169 separate authorities evaluating commercial kitchens: 62 in California, 36 in Oregon, 39 in Washington, 17 in Nevada, and 15 in Arizona. Each one sets its own methodology.

That''s not a technicality. It changes what a violation costs you, how often you''re inspected, what gets published where your customers can see it, and what triggers a return visit.

Most compliance advice is written as though one rulebook exists. It doesn''t, and following generic advice means preparing for an inspection that isn''t the one you''re going to get.

**The four families of evaluation**

**Letter grades.** A county converts findings into a letter and requires you to post it. The consequence is public and immediate — it''s on your door before you''ve had a chance to explain anything.

**Numeric.** A point total, usually starting from a maximum and deducting for findings. The methodology matters enormously here: what a given violation deducts, whether repeat findings stack, whether certain violations supersede others.

**Pass/fail or in-compliance.** No gradation. You met the standard or you didn''t. Simpler, but it compresses a lot of information out — a kitchen with one minor finding and a kitchen with eleven can land in the same bucket.

**Nothing published.** Some counties inspect and document but don''t publish results publicly at all. Your record exists; it just isn''t a public-facing consequence.

**Why the same violation carries different weight**

This is where operators get caught. A temperature finding that costs a small deduction in one county might be classified as a major violation in the county next door, triggering a re-inspection and a public posting.

If you operate across county lines, you cannot carry one mental model. What satisfied one inspector generates a correction notice at your other location, and the staff at that location will reasonably believe they''re being held to a different standard — because they are.

**What blending destroys**

The temptation is to average all of this into one number. Resist it.

The moment you convert your county''s actual output into a proprietary figure, you''ve introduced drift between what you''re looking at and what your authority will actually do. And the drift always shows up at the worst time — because you were reading a number that felt reassuring while your county was applying a methodology that didn''t agree with it.

EvidLY reproduces your county''s methodology rather than replacing it. If your county publishes a letter grade, you see a letter grade. If it publishes a number, you see the number, calculated the way your county calculates it — including supersession, stacking, and exceptions. If your county publishes nothing, we don''t invent something.

Your authority is the only authority. Anything else is a comfortable fiction.

**How to find your own county''s methodology**

Most county environmental health departments publish their scoring methodology, their violation classifications, and often their annual enforcement summary. It''s usually a PDF, it''s usually not linked prominently, and it''s usually worth an hour of your time.

Search your county''s environmental health department plus "inspection methodology" or "violation classification." If you operate in more than one county, do it for each — and then compare them, because the comparison is the education.

Or take a tour and we''ll show you yours with your own kitchen''s information in about twenty minutes.

Every kitchen that runs right is a family that keeps its livelihood. That''s the why behind all of this.

**Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.**

EvidLY · getevidly.com · (855) 384-3591

*Editor note: verify each county methodology reference against the county''s published documentation before publish. Do not name a county''s specific methodology without a primary source.*', 'getevidly.com/blog', 'Blog post', '[copy_state: Final] [source: EvidLY-Blog]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'EvidLY' AND c.channel_label = 'Blog' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-08-11' AND c.title = 'Letter grade, number, or nothing: how your county actually evaluates your kitchen');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Why EvidLY will never give you a single compliance number', 'Blog', '2026-08-18', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'There''s a version of this that would be easier to sell. One number, big and clean, at the top of a dashboard. Green is good. Red is bad. Executives love it.

We''re not going to build that, and it''s worth explaining why.

**Two authorities, two bodies of law**

Food safety in a commercial kitchen is governed by your county health department, working from your state''s food code. Fire safety is governed by your fire authority, working primarily from NFPA 96 and related standards.

These are not two departments within one agency. They''re separate authorities with separate legal foundations, separate inspectors, separate inspection schedules, and separate enforcement consequences. They generally don''t share a system, and in many counties they don''t share information at all.

Averaging their outputs implies a relationship that doesn''t exist.

**What the average actually hides**

Say your food safety is excellent and your fire safety has a real problem — an exhaust system past its interval, a suppression service that lapsed, no documentation of either.

Average those together and you land somewhere comfortable. Comfortable enough that nobody digs.

That''s not a neutral outcome. The number didn''t just fail to help you. It actively concealed the exposure, and it did so in a format designed to make you stop looking.

Now reverse it. Fire safety immaculate, food safety drifting. Same average, completely different problem, and the same failure to surface it.

One figure cannot represent two independent risks. It can only obscure whichever one is worse.

**"But I need something for the board"**

Fair. Multi-unit leadership does need a rollup, and refusing to provide any summary at all isn''t helpful either.

The answer is two views side by side rather than one figure. Food safety across your locations, fire safety across your locations. A leadership team can absolutely absorb two columns, and two columns tell them something actionable: which risk to fund this quarter.

One column tells them everything is basically fine, which is not information.

**The dual-authority case**

Some kitchens operate under two authorities simultaneously — a county health department and a federal or campus authority, for instance. Two rulebooks running at once in one building.

It sounds like an edge case. It''s actually a preview of what any multi-county operator faces once they cross a line. The lesson is the same: what works is a view that respects each authority on its own terms, not a synthesis that respects neither.

**What to ask a vendor claiming one number**

Ask which authority produced it.

They can''t answer, because no authority did. It''s derived. And once something is derived, you have to ask what it was derived from, what weighting was applied, and whether that weighting has any relationship to how your county actually enforces.

Usually the honest answer is that it doesn''t. It was designed to look good on a screen.

**What we do instead**

Your food safety, evaluated against your county''s methodology, exactly as your county produces it. Your fire safety, evaluated against your fire authority''s requirements. Both visible. Never combined.

If that''s a harder sell than one green number, so be it. Clarity over comfort.

You can''t reduce a risk you can''t see — and a blended figure''s entire function, whether intended or not, is to make sure you can''t see it.

I built this so kitchen leaders would stop being surprised by the ordinary. That''s the why.

**Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.**

EvidLY · getevidly.com · (855) 384-3591', 'getevidly.com/blog', 'Blog post', '[copy_state: Final] [source: EvidLY-Blog]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'EvidLY' AND c.channel_label = 'Blog' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-08-18' AND c.title = 'Why EvidLY will never give you a single compliance number');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'The five documents that decide a commercial kitchen insurance claim', 'Blog', '2026-08-25', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'The fire is survivable. For most kitchens, the denied claim isn''t.

When a carrier reviews a commercial kitchen loss, they aren''t assessing whether you ran a good operation. They''re assessing whether you can demonstrate it. Those are different questions, and the second one is answered entirely by documents.

Here are the five that usually decide it.

**1. Hood cleaning records**

Not the last invoice. The history — enough of it to establish that your service interval matched what your cooking volume requires.

A carrier looking at three services across eighteen months on a kitchen that should be quarterly is looking at a documented gap. What the record needs: dates, the areas actually accessed by name (filters, hood interior, plenum, horizontal duct, vertical duct, fan housing), accumulation measured, photographs before and after, and the qualification of whoever performed the work.

An invoice with a date on it is not a service record.

**2. Suppression system service history**

Your suppression system has a required service interval and a service life. Both are documented in the applicable standard, and both are things a carrier can check against your records.

The common failure is a lapse of a few months that nobody noticed because nothing broke. Nothing breaking is not the same as being in service — and after a loss, the gap is visible in a way it never was before.

**3. Inspection history**

Your health department and fire authority inspection records. Findings, corrections, re-inspections, and the dates of each.

This matters less for the findings themselves than for the pattern. A kitchen with findings that were promptly corrected reads very differently from a kitchen with the same finding recurring across three visits. The first is an operation that responds. The second is an operation with a known issue and no evidence of remediation.

**4. Employee training logs**

Certification dates, expiration dates, and what training actually covered.

Fire safety training tends to be the weak spot. Food handler certification is required and therefore tracked. Suppression system activation procedures, extinguisher use, shutdown sequence — often trained verbally during onboarding and documented nowhere.

If your training exists only as institutional memory, it does not exist for claim purposes.

**5. Photographic evidence of condition**

Timestamped images showing the state of the kitchen and its systems over time. Not staged marketing photos. Actual condition, dated, ideally captured as part of routine service and inspection.

This is the least common of the five and the most persuasive when present. It converts a documentation argument into an observable fact.

**The part everyone gets wrong**

Most kitchens have some version of all five. The records exist.

They exist in a vendor''s truck, in an email thread from two years ago, in a binder in an office that may also have burned, and on a former manager''s phone.

Existing somewhere and being producible are not the same thing, and only one of them helps.

Here''s the test. Right now, without preparation: can you produce twelve months of hood cleaning records, your suppression service history, your last three inspection reports, current training certifications, and dated photographs of your system — in under ten minutes, from your phone?

If not, you don''t have a proof chain. You have documents in various places, which is what everyone has, and which is why claims get denied.

**What to do about it**

Consolidate before you need it. The consolidation is not the hard part. Remembering to do it while nothing is wrong is the hard part, which is exactly why it doesn''t happen.

That''s the gap EvidLY was built to close — proof kept where you can produce it, dated, retrievable, tied to the county and authority that will actually ask.

I''ve watched a fully covered loss become a denied claim because five documents didn''t exist in retrievable form. The fire was survivable. The denial wasn''t. That was a family''s livelihood, gone on paperwork.

That''s the why.

**Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.**

EvidLY · getevidly.com · (855) 384-3591

*Editor note: verify all references to suppression service intervals and standard requirements against the current published standards before publish. Cite exact section numbers. Do not paraphrase requirement language.*', 'getevidly.com/blog', 'Blog post', '[copy_state: Final] [source: EvidLY-Blog]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'EvidLY' AND c.channel_label = 'Blog' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-08-25' AND c.title = 'The five documents that decide a commercial kitchen insurance claim');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Reading the signal: the equipment drift that precedes failure by 90 days', 'Blog', '2026-09-01', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'A walk-in doesn''t fail on a Tuesday out of nowhere. It drifts, and the drift is measurable for months before anything stops working.

The kitchens that get blindsided by equipment failure aren''t careless. They simply had nothing reading the signal — and the signal is quiet by design. Nothing about a slow drift announces itself the way a breakdown does.

Here''s what to watch, and what each pattern actually means.

**Recovery time is the leading indicator**

Most kitchens watch temperature. Temperature is a lagging indicator — by the time your walk-in can''t hold temp, you''re already in the failure.

Recovery time is the leading one. How long does the unit take to return to setpoint after the door closes? A unit that used to recover in four minutes and now takes seven is telling you something, and it''s telling you months before it can''t recover at all.

That drift is invisible if you''re only looking at whether the current reading is in range. It''s obvious if you''re looking at the trend.

**Cycling frequency**

A compressor that cycles more often than it did last quarter is working harder to do the same job. Could be a refrigerant issue, a door seal, a condenser that needs cleaning, or the early stage of compressor wear.

All four are cheaper to address now than after the failure — and all four look identical from inside the kitchen, which is why the trend matters more than any single reading.

**Fryer thermostat drift**

Fryer thermostats drift gradually and predictably. The oil runs a few degrees off setpoint, then a few more.

The immediate cost is food quality and oil life. The eventual cost is a thermostat that fails open, which is a fire event, not a maintenance event.

Drift here is measurable well in advance if anyone is recording actual versus setpoint rather than assuming the dial is telling the truth.

**Exhaust fan bearing heat**

This one crosses from equipment into fire safety.

A fan bearing that runs hot will seize. Before it does, the belt often begins to slip, and a slipping belt means the fan turns slower than rated — which means less air moved, which means grease vapor condensing earlier in the duct run than the system was designed for.

So a mechanical issue on the roof in March produces heavier duct accumulation in June, on a kitchen whose cleaning interval was set assuming normal airflow. The interval is now wrong and nothing inside the kitchen said so.

**Suppression system service life**

Not a drift signal exactly, but the same category of avoidable surprise: suppression systems have a required service interval and a service life, both documented in the applicable standard.

The failure mode is a lapse nobody noticed, because a suppression system that has never discharged gives you no feedback whatsoever about whether it''s in service.

**Planned versus emergency: the actual difference**

The reason any of this matters is cost, and the gap is bigger than most operators account for.

A planned repair is a scheduled visit, standard rate, at a time you chose, with the option to get a second quote.

An emergency is after-hours rate, whatever technician is available, no leverage on price — plus product loss if it''s refrigeration, plus potential closure hours, plus a health department conversation you didn''t want to have, all landing in the same week.

Same component. Same failure. Two completely different numbers, decided entirely by whether anyone was reading the signal three months earlier.

**Where EvidLY fits**

This is the Predict half of Predict, Reduce, Prove. Identifying the pattern early enough that you''re choosing the timing instead of reacting to it.

Not a guess and not a maintenance schedule pulled from a manual — the actual trend from your actual equipment, surfaced early enough to matter.

Every kitchen that runs right is a family that keeps its livelihood. Giving kitchen leaders enough warning to protect that is the whole point.

**Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.**

EvidLY · getevidly.com · (855) 384-3591

*Editor note: verify all references to suppression service intervals and equipment standards against current published standards before publish.*', 'getevidly.com/blog', 'Blog post', '[copy_state: Final] [source: EvidLY-Blog]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'EvidLY' AND c.channel_label = 'Blog' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-09-01' AND c.title = 'Reading the signal: the equipment drift that precedes failure by 90 days');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Prove: the capability nobody builds until they need it', 'Blog', '2026-09-08', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'Every kitchen leader I''ve met can describe how they run their kitchen. Very few can demonstrate it — on demand, to someone skeptical, from records, without a week of preparation.

Those are different capabilities, and only one of them is evaluated.

**The asymmetry**

An inspector, a carrier, or a buyer doing due diligence is not assessing whether you run a good operation. They''re assessing whether you can show it.

That means a kitchen that did the work and can''t produce the evidence occupies the same evidentiary position as a kitchen that didn''t do the work.

That''s harsh. It''s also the actual standard in all three contexts, and it doesn''t soften because the operator was sincere.

**Why the gap exists**

Not negligence. Structure.

Proving requires assembling evidence while nothing is wrong — which is precisely when there''s no pressure to do it and no reward for having done it. The work is invisible until the moment it isn''t, and by then it can''t be done retroactively.

Meanwhile the records genuinely exist. In a vendor''s system. In an email thread from two years ago. In a binder in an office. On a former manager''s phone.

Existing and being producible are different states. Only the second one is a capability.

**The three audiences**

**The inspector** asks whether requirements were met and can be shown. Interval history, scope, corrections after prior findings.

**The carrier** asks whether coverage conditions were maintained and can be demonstrated. Everything the inspector wants plus measurement and photographic evidence.

**The buyer** asks whether the systems they''re acquiring were maintained. Multi-year history with documented scope. A gap here is a diligence finding, and diligence findings adjust price.

Build to the carrier standard and you satisfy all three, because it''s the strictest.

**What Prove actually requires**

Five things, none of them exotic.

**Consolidation.** One place, not four. The location matters less than the singularity.

**Dating.** Every artifact carries the date it describes, not the date it was filed.

**Scope.** Documentation says what was covered, by name. "Serviced" is not scope.

**Condition.** Measurement or photographic evidence of state, not just an assertion that work occurred.

**Retrievability.** Producible in minutes, from anywhere, without calling anyone.

That last one is where most attempts fail. Operations build the first four and store the result somewhere that requires a person, a password, or a physical location to access.

**The ten-minute test**

Right now, without preparation, from your phone.

Twelve months of hood cleaning documentation with scope and condition. Suppression service history. Last three inspection reports with correction evidence. Current training certifications. Dated photographs of system condition.

If you can''t, you don''t have Prove. You have documents, which is what everyone has.

**Where this sits in the framework**

Predict, Reduce, Prove — in that order, and the order is the argument.

Predict identifies what''s coming while there''s still time to choose a response. Reduce lowers probability and consequence with the time prediction bought. Prove converts both into something defensible.

Prediction without reduction is anxiety. Reduction without proof is invisible — you did the work and can''t show it. Proof without the first two is theater.

All three, in sequence, is a kitchen that knew what was coming, acted on it, and can show its work.

**Why not "prevent"**

Because prevention isn''t available.

You cannot prevent every lapse in a commercial kitchen. Equipment ages, people turn over, conditions change. Anyone promising prevention is promising something that doesn''t exist — and the promise works precisely because it''s what customers want to hear.

Reduce is honest. It''s achievable, it''s measurable, and when it doesn''t fully work, the customer isn''t holding a guarantee that was never real.

Small word. It''s the difference between a claim I can defend and one I can''t.

Every kitchen that runs right is a family that keeps its livelihood. Proving the work is how that protection survives contact with someone who has no reason to take your word for it.

**Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.**

