INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Why your county scores your kitchen differently than the county next door (8-10 min)', 'YouTube', '2026-08-12', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'TITLE: Why Your County Grades Your Kitchen Differently Than The County Next Door
UPLOAD: Wednesday · THUMBNAIL: two identical kitchens, gold "A" vs "91", navy bar "SAME KITCHEN?"

── SCRIPT ──

[COLD OPEN — on camera]
Two kitchens. Same menu. Same equipment. Same violations found on the same day.

One gets an A in the window. One gets a 91 posted online. One gets nothing published at all.

The difference isn''t the kitchen. It''s the county.

[TITLE CARD]

[ON CAMERA]
I''m Arthur Haggerty, founder of EvidLY. Before this I spent twenty-five years in consulting helping large organizations see things they couldn''t see, and then I spent years in commercial kitchens as a service vendor.

And the thing that struck me hardest in those kitchens was how much confident advice exists that''s written as though there''s one set of rules.

There isn''t.

[GRAPHIC — five-state map]
Across the five states we cover, there are 169 separate authorities evaluating commercial kitchens. Sixty-two in California. Thirty-six in Oregon. Thirty-nine in Washington. Seventeen in Nevada. Fifteen in Arizona.

Each one sets its own methodology.

[ON CAMERA]
That''s not a technicality. It changes what a violation costs you, how often you get inspected, what gets published where your customers can see it, and what brings an inspector back.

Let me walk you through the four families.

[GRAPHIC — model 1]
Letter grades. Your county converts findings into a letter and requires you to post it. The consequence is public and it''s immediate — it''s on your door before you''ve had any chance to explain.

[GRAPHIC — model 2]
Numeric. A point total, usually deducting from a maximum. And the methodology underneath matters enormously — what a given violation deducts, whether repeat findings stack, whether some violations supersede others.

Two counties both using numbers can produce completely different results from identical findings.

[GRAPHIC — model 3]
Pass/fail, or in-compliance. No gradation. You met the standard or you didn''t.

Simpler — but it compresses information out. A kitchen with one minor finding and a kitchen with eleven can land in the same bucket.

[GRAPHIC — model 4]
And some counties inspect, document, and publish nothing publicly at all. Your record exists. It just isn''t a public consequence.

[ON CAMERA — the key beat]
Here''s where operators get caught.

The same violation carries different weight in different counties.

A temperature finding that costs a small deduction in one county might be classified as a major violation forty miles away — triggering a re-inspection and a public posting.

If you run locations across county lines, you cannot carry one mental model. What satisfied the inspector at location one generates a correction notice at location two. And your staff at location two will reasonably believe they''re being held to a different standard.

They are.

[ON CAMERA — the argument]
So the temptation is obvious. Average it all into one number. One clean figure, top of the dashboard.

Don''t.

The moment you convert your county''s actual output into a proprietary figure, you''ve introduced drift between what you''re looking at and what your authority is actually going to do.

And drift always shows up at the worst possible time — because you were reading something reassuring while your county was applying a methodology that didn''t agree with it.

[ON CAMERA]
That''s why EvidLY reproduces your county''s methodology instead of replacing it.

Letter grade county? You see a letter grade. Numeric? You see the number, calculated the way your county calculates it — including supersession, stacking, exceptions. County publishes nothing? We don''t invent something.

Your authority is the only authority.

[CLOSE — practical]
If you want to do this yourself, here''s how.

Search your county''s environmental health department plus "inspection methodology" or "violation classification." It''s usually a PDF, usually not linked prominently, and usually worth an hour.

If you run in more than one county, do it for each — and then compare them. The comparison is the education.

Or take a tour and we''ll show you yours, with your own kitchen''s information, in about twenty minutes. Link''s in the description.

Every kitchen that runs right is a family that keeps its livelihood. That''s the why behind all of this.

Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.

[END CARD] EvidLY · getevidly.com · (855) 384-3591

── DESCRIPTION ──
There is no single health department. There are 169 across California, Oregon, Washington, Nevada, and Arizona — each evaluating commercial kitchens on its own methodology. Arthur Haggerty breaks down the four evaluation models, why the same violation carries different weight county to county, and why averaging it into one number hides your real exposure. Free resources, no login: getevidly.com/resources

── TAGS ──
restaurant health inspection, county health department, food safety compliance, commercial kitchen, restaurant grading

── SHORTS CUT ──
"There is no the health department — there are 169." Through the same-violation-different-weight beat. 50 seconds.

── EDITOR NOTE ──
Do not name a specific county''s methodology on screen without a verified primary source.', 'getevidly.com', 'YouTube — full script', '[copy_state: Final] [source: EvidLY-YouTube]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'EvidLY' AND c.channel_label = 'YouTube' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-08-12' AND c.title = 'Why your county scores your kitchen differently than the county next door (8-10 min)');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'The single compliance score is a lie — and what to use instead (7-9 min)', 'YouTube', '2026-08-19', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'TITLE: Why A Single Compliance Score Is A Lie (And What To Use Instead)
UPLOAD: Wednesday · THUMBNAIL: hard split teal/coral with gold divider, "NEVER BLEND"

── SCRIPT ──

[COLD OPEN — on camera]
If a vendor shows you one compliance number, ask them which authority produced it.

They can''t answer. Because no authority did.

[TITLE CARD]

[ON CAMERA]
Arthur Haggerty, EvidLY. There''s a version of what I build that would be easier to sell — one big clean number at the top of a dashboard, green is good, red is bad.

We''re not building that. Here''s why.

[GRAPHIC — two authorities]
Food safety in your kitchen is governed by your county health department, working from your state''s food code.

Fire safety is governed by your fire authority, working primarily from NFPA 96 and related standards.

These are not two departments in one agency. Separate authorities. Separate legal foundations. Separate inspectors. Separate schedules. Separate consequences. In a lot of counties they don''t share information at all.

Averaging their outputs implies a relationship that doesn''t exist.

[ON CAMERA — the worked example]
Let me show you what the average actually does. This is the part that matters.

Say your food safety is excellent. Genuinely strong. And your fire safety has a real problem — exhaust system past interval, suppression service lapsed, no documentation of either.

[GRAPHIC — the math]
Average those. You land somewhere comfortable.

Comfortable enough that nobody digs.

[ON CAMERA]
That''s not a neutral outcome. The number didn''t just fail to help you.

It concealed the exposure. And it did it in a format specifically designed to make you stop looking.

Now flip it. Fire safety immaculate, food safety drifting. Same average. Completely different problem. Same failure to surface it.

One figure can''t represent two independent risks. It can only hide whichever one is worse.

[ON CAMERA — objection handling]
Now, the fair objection: "I have a board. I have a regional VP. I need a rollup."

Absolutely. And refusing to summarize anything isn''t helpful either.

The answer is two views side by side, not one figure. Food safety across your locations. Fire safety across your locations.

Any leadership team can absorb two columns. And two columns tell them something actionable — which risk to fund this quarter.

One column tells them everything is basically fine. That''s not information. That''s reassurance.

[ON CAMERA — dual authority]
There''s a version of this that makes it obvious.

Some kitchens operate under two authorities at once — a county health department and a separate federal or campus authority. Two rulebooks running simultaneously in one building.

Sounds like an edge case. It''s actually a preview of what any multi-county operator faces the moment they cross a line.

And the lesson is identical: what works is a view that respects each authority on its own terms. Not a synthesis that respects neither.

[CLOSE — the test]
So here''s your question for any vendor selling you one number.

Which authority produced this?

They''ll tell you it''s derived. Fine — derived from what? Weighted how? And does that weighting have any relationship to how your county actually enforces?

Usually the honest answer is no. It was designed to look good on a screen.

What we do instead: your food safety, evaluated against your county''s methodology, exactly as your county produces it. Your fire safety, evaluated against your fire authority''s requirements. Both visible. Never combined.

Harder sell than one green number. I''ll take it.

Clarity over comfort. You can''t reduce a risk you can''t see — and a blended figure''s whole function, intended or not, is making sure you can''t see it.

Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.

[END CARD] EvidLY · getevidly.com · (855) 384-3591

