INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'The 5 documents that decide your insurance claim (10-12 min)', 'YouTube', '2026-08-26', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'TITLE: The 5 Documents That Decide Your Commercial Kitchen Insurance Claim
UPLOAD: Wednesday · THUMBNAIL: five numbered doc icons on navy, gold "5", "PAID OR DENIED"

── SCRIPT ──

[COLD OPEN — on camera]
The fire is survivable.

For most kitchens, the denied claim isn''t.

[TITLE CARD]

[ON CAMERA]
Arthur Haggerty, EvidLY. When a carrier reviews a commercial kitchen loss, they are not assessing whether you ran a good operation.

They''re assessing whether you can demonstrate it.

Those are completely different questions. And the second one is answered entirely by documents.

Five of them, usually.

[DOC 1]
One. Hood cleaning records.

Not the last invoice — the history. Enough to establish that your service interval actually matched what your cooking volume requires.

A carrier looking at three services across eighteen months, on a kitchen that should be quarterly, is looking at a documented gap.

What the record needs: dates. Areas accessed by name — filters, hood interior, plenum, horizontal duct, vertical duct, fan housing. Accumulation measured. Photographs before and after. And the qualification of whoever did the work.

An invoice with a date on it is not a service record.

[DOC 2]
Two. Suppression system service history.

Your suppression system has a required service interval and a service life. Both documented in the applicable standard. Both checkable against your records.

The common failure here is a lapse of a few months that nobody noticed, because nothing broke.

Nothing breaking is not the same as being in service. And after a loss, that gap is visible in a way it never was before.

[DOC 3]
Three. Inspection history.

Health department and fire authority records. Findings, corrections, re-inspections, dates.

This matters less for the findings themselves than for the pattern.

A kitchen with findings that got corrected promptly reads completely differently from a kitchen with the same finding recurring across three visits. First one is an operation that responds. Second one is an operation with a known issue and no evidence of remediation.

[DOC 4]
Four. Employee training logs.

Certification dates, expiration dates, what the training actually covered.

Fire safety training is almost always the weak spot. Food handler certification is required, so it gets tracked. Suppression activation procedure, extinguisher use, shutdown sequence — usually trained verbally at onboarding and documented nowhere.

If your training exists only as institutional memory, it does not exist for claim purposes.

[DOC 5]
Five. Photographic evidence of condition.

Timestamped images showing the state of your kitchen and its systems over time. Not staged photos. Actual condition, dated, ideally captured as part of routine service.

Least common of the five. Most persuasive when it''s there — because it turns a documentation argument into an observable fact.

[ON CAMERA — the real problem]
Now here''s the part everybody gets wrong.

Most kitchens have some version of all five. The records exist.

They exist in a vendor''s truck. In an email thread from two years ago. In a binder in an office that may also have burned. And on a former manager''s phone.

Existing somewhere and being producible are not the same thing. Only one of them helps.

[ON CAMERA — the test]
So here''s the test. Right now, no preparation.

Can you produce twelve months of hood cleaning records, your suppression service history, your last three inspection reports, current training certifications, and dated photographs of your system — in under ten minutes, from your phone?

If not, you don''t have a proof chain. You have documents in various places.

Which is what everyone has. And which is why claims get denied.

[CLOSE]
The consolidation isn''t the hard part. Remembering to do it while nothing is wrong — that''s the hard part. Which is exactly why it doesn''t happen.

That''s the gap EvidLY closes. Proof kept where you can produce it, dated, retrievable, tied to the county and the authority that will actually ask.

I''ve watched a fully covered loss become a denied claim because five documents didn''t exist in retrievable form. The fire was survivable. The denial wasn''t.

That was a family''s livelihood, gone on paperwork. That''s the why.

Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.

[END CARD] EvidLY · getevidly.com · (855) 384-3591

