INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Prove: the capability nobody builds until they need it (8-10 min)', 'YouTube', '2026-09-09', 'drafted', 'EvidLY', 'TITLE: Doing The Work And Proving It Are Two Different Things
UPLOAD: Wednesday · THUMBNAIL: three stacked bars PREDICT/REDUCE/PROVE, gold on navy

── SCRIPT ──

[COLD OPEN — on camera]
Every kitchen leader I''ve met can describe how they run their kitchen.

Very few can demonstrate it. On demand. To someone skeptical. From records. Without a week of preparation.

Those are two different capabilities. Only one of them gets evaluated.

[TITLE]

[ON CAMERA]
Arthur Haggerty, EvidLY. We''ve spent this month on Predict and Reduce. This is the third one — and it''s the one everyone ignores until it''s the only thing that matters.

[ON CAMERA — the asymmetry]
Here''s the uncomfortable part.

An inspector, a carrier, or a buyer''s due diligence team is not assessing whether you run a good operation.

They''re assessing whether you can show it.

Which means a kitchen that did the work and can''t produce the evidence sits in the same position as a kitchen that didn''t do the work at all.

That''s harsh. It''s the actual standard in all three contexts. And it doesn''t soften because you were sincere.

[ON CAMERA — why the gap exists]
Now, why does this gap exist almost everywhere? It isn''t negligence.

It''s structure.

Proving requires assembling evidence while nothing is wrong — which is exactly when there''s no pressure to do it, and no reward for having done it.

The work is invisible until the moment it isn''t. And by then you can''t do it retroactively.

Meanwhile the records genuinely exist. In a vendor''s system. An email thread from two years ago. A binder in the office. A former manager''s phone.

Existing and being producible are different states. Only the second one is a capability.

[GRAPHIC — three audiences]
Three audiences, three questions.

The inspector: were requirements met, and can you show it. Interval history, scope, corrections after prior findings.

The carrier: were coverage conditions maintained, and can you demonstrate it. Everything the inspector wants, plus measurement and photographic evidence.

The buyer: were the systems we''re acquiring maintained. Multi-year history with documented scope. A gap here is a diligence finding — and diligence findings adjust price.

Build to the carrier standard and you satisfy all three. It''s the strictest.

[GRAPHIC — five requirements]
What Prove actually requires. Five things, none exotic.

Consolidation. One place, not four. Where matters less than that it''s singular.

Dating. Every artifact carries the date it describes — not the date it was filed.

Scope. Documentation says what was covered, by name. "Serviced" is not a scope.

Condition. Measurement or photographs of state. Not just an assertion that work occurred.

And retrievability. Producible in minutes, from anywhere, without calling anyone.

[ON CAMERA]
That last one is where most attempts die.

Operations build the first four and then store the result somewhere that requires a person, a password, or a physical location to reach.

[ON CAMERA — the test]
So here''s the test. Right now. No preparation. From your phone.

Twelve months of hood cleaning documentation with scope and condition. Suppression service history. Last three inspection reports with correction evidence. Current training certifications. Dated photographs of system condition.

If you can''t — you don''t have Prove. You have documents. Which is what everyone has.

[ON CAMERA — the framework]
Predict. Reduce. Prove. In that order, and the order is the argument.

Predict identifies what''s coming while there''s still time to choose a response.

Reduce lowers probability and consequence, using the time prediction bought.

Prove converts both into something defensible.

Prediction without reduction is anxiety. Reduction without proof is invisible — you did the work and can''t show it. Proof without the first two is theater.

All three in sequence is a kitchen that knew, acted, and can show its work.

[CLOSE — why not prevent]
One last thing, and it''s a word choice I get asked about.

I won''t say "prevent."

Because prevention isn''t available. You cannot prevent every lapse in a commercial kitchen. Equipment ages. People turn over. Conditions change.

Anyone promising prevention is promising something that doesn''t exist — and the promise works precisely because it''s what customers want to hear.

Reduce is honest. Achievable, measurable. And when it doesn''t fully work, you aren''t holding a guarantee that was never real.

Small word. It''s the difference between a claim I can defend and one I can''t.

Predict the lapse. Reduce the cost.

[END CARD] EvidLY · getevidly.com · (855) 384-3591

── DESCRIPTION ──
An inspector, a carrier, and a buyer''s due diligence team aren''t assessing whether you run a good kitchen — they''re assessing whether you can show it. Arthur Haggerty on the third pillar of Predict, Reduce, Prove: what proving actually requires, the ten-minute test most operations fail, and why "prevent" is a word he won''t use.

── TAGS ──
restaurant compliance, food safety documentation, commercial kitchen, restaurant risk management, due diligence

