INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Issue 1: 169 rulebooks, not one', 'Email', '2026-08-14', 'drafted', 'Stovio Advisors', 'THE COMMERCIAL KITCHEN STANDARD
Friday Brief — Issue 1

—

**THIS WEEK**

We published two pieces on county variance, and they''re really one argument: the phrase "the health department" describes something that does not exist.

Across California, Oregon, Washington, Nevada, and Arizona there are 169 separate authorities evaluating commercial kitchens. Sixty-two in California alone. Each sets its own methodology — letter grade, numeric, pass/fail, or nothing published at all.

The consequence isn''t academic. The same violation carries different weight across a county line. Inspection frequency differs. What triggers a re-inspection differs. An operator running two locations in two counties is running two different compliance programs whether they know it or not.

→ *What Los Angeles County inspectors look for in the first 90 seconds*
→ *Riverside, San Bernardino, Orange: three counties, three rulebooks*

—

**REGULATORY PULSE**

[One verified item this week. Primary source cited inline. If nothing verifiable, this section is omitted rather than filled.]

—

**FROM THE FIELD**

An observation from a week of commercial kitchen service across Northern California — one thing seen, what it indicated, what it cost or saved. No client names.

—

**A NOTE**

I started this publication because I kept reading confident compliance advice written as though one rulebook exists. Operators act on that advice, and then get held to something else entirely.

Everything here is sourced to published primary material. If I can''t verify it, I don''t publish it. That''s the whole editorial standard.

Arthur Haggerty
Stovio Advisors

