INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Issue 2: Two authorities, one kitchen', 'Email', '2026-08-21', 'drafted', 'Stovio Advisors', 'THE COMMERCIAL KITCHEN STANDARD
Friday Brief — Issue 2

—

**THIS WEEK**

Food safety and fire safety are governed by two separate authorities working from two separate bodies of law. They rarely share a system and in many counties they don''t share information at all.

This week we looked at kitchens where that separation is unmistakable — operations answering to a county health department and a second authority simultaneously. Two rulebooks running at once, in one building, every day.

It reads like an edge case. It''s actually a preview of what any operator faces the moment they cross a county line.

We also examined which evaluation model produces better outcomes. Letter grades, numeric, pass/fail — the model a county chose shapes operator behavior more than most of the regulation inside it.

→ *The dual-jurisdiction kitchen: when two authorities govern one building*
→ *Letter grade, numeric, pass/fail: which model produces fewer failures?*

—

**REGULATORY PULSE**

[Verified item. Primary source cited.]

—

**FROM THE FIELD**

[Observation from the service week.]

—

**A NOTE**

The instinct to simplify two authorities into one number is understandable and wrong. It produces a figure no authority would recognize, and it hides whichever risk is worse.

Arthur Haggerty
Stovio Advisors

