INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Issue 3: Paid or denied', 'Email', '2026-08-28', 'drafted', 'Stovio Advisors', 'THE COMMERCIAL KITCHEN STANDARD
Friday Brief — Issue 3

—

**THIS WEEK — FLAGSHIP**

The difference between a paid commercial kitchen claim and a denied one is usually five documents.

Hood cleaning records. Suppression service history. Inspection history. Employee training logs. Photographic evidence of condition.

Most kitchens have some version of all five. Very few can produce them. That distinction — having versus producing — is where claims are actually decided, and it''s the subject of our longest piece to date.

We also looked at what triggers a carrier loss control inspection and what those inspections actually check. A loss control visit is information, not accusation — but it moves your premium either way.

→ *The five documentation gaps that turn a covered fire into a denied claim*
→ *What triggers a carrier loss control inspection — and what they find*

—

**REGULATORY PULSE**

[Verified item. Primary source cited.]

—

**FROM THE FIELD**

[Observation from the service week.]

—

**A NOTE**

Everything in this week''s flagship is sourced to published material — standards, policy forms, and published case captions. Where a pattern is inferred rather than documented, we say so.

That restraint costs us some sharper claims. It''s the reason the publication is worth reading.

Arthur Haggerty
Stovio Advisors

