INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Issue 4: The 90-day warning', 'Email', '2026-09-04', 'drafted', 'Stovio Advisors', 'THE COMMERCIAL KITCHEN STANDARD
Friday Brief — Issue 4

—

**THIS WEEK**

Commercial kitchen equipment drifts measurably for months before it fails. Recovery time, cycling frequency, thermostat drift, bearing heat.

The signal is quiet by design — nothing about a slow drift announces itself the way a breakdown does. This week we mapped the specific patterns and, more usefully, the cost difference between catching them and not.

We also revisited cuisine type and grease accumulation. Two kitchens on the same quarterly schedule can carry wildly different fire loads, and NFPA 96 says so explicitly. Most intervals are set by habit instead.

→ *Equipment drift: the 90-day signals that precede commercial kitchen failure*
→ *Cuisine type and grease accumulation: when your interval is wrong*

—

**REGULATORY PULSE**

[Verified item. Primary source cited.]

—

**FROM THE FIELD**

[Observation from the service week.]

—

**A NOTE**

A month of this publication. The thing I keep returning to: almost every failure we document was legible in advance to somebody who knew where to look.

That''s not a comfort. It''s an argument for looking.

Arthur Haggerty
Stovio Advisors

