INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'What your county actually publishes about restaurants', 'Email', '2026-08-12', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'Every county handles restaurant inspection results differently — and most people, including a lot of restaurant owners, have no idea what their own county publishes.

Some post a letter grade you''ll see on the door. Some publish a numeric result online. Some publish a pass/fail status. Some publish nothing at all to the public.

ScoreTable exists to make that findable. One page per county across California, Oregon, Nevada, Washington, and Arizona — what your county evaluates, how it evaluates, and where the public record lives.

We don''t rate anyone. We don''t average anything. We don''t produce our own number. We show you what the county itself produces, and where to find it.

That distinction matters. The moment anyone invents a rating on top of official data, you''re reading something the county never said.

The Score Behind Every Table.

ScoreTable', 'Find your county', 'Email', '[copy_state: Final] [source: ScoreTable-Email]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'ScoreTable' AND c.channel_label = 'Email' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-08-12' AND c.title = 'What your county actually publishes about restaurants');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Two authorities decide whether a kitchen is safe. Most people only know one.', 'Email', '2026-08-19', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'When people think about restaurant safety, they think about the health department.

That''s half of it.

Food safety is governed by your county health department. Fire safety in a commercial kitchen — the exhaust system, the suppression system, the grease that accumulates above the cooking line — is governed by your fire authority, under a completely separate standard.

Different inspectors. Different schedules. Different records. In many counties, different databases that never speak to each other.

Which means the public information most people can find covers one of two systems that decide whether a kitchen is actually safe.

ScoreTable documents what each county publishes, county by county, for the five states we cover. Where the public record is thin, we say so rather than filling the gap with something invented.

The Score Behind Every Table.

ScoreTable', 'Find your county', 'Email', '[copy_state: Final] [source: ScoreTable-Email]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'ScoreTable' AND c.channel_label = 'Email' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-08-19' AND c.title = 'Two authorities decide whether a kitchen is safe. Most people only know one.');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Why the same violation means different things in different counties', 'Email', '2026-08-26', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'A temperature finding in one county might cost a small deduction. Forty miles away, the same finding might be classified as major, trigger a re-inspection, and get published where customers can see it.

Same observation. Same kitchen conditions. Different consequence.

That''s not inconsistency in enforcement — it''s 169 separate authorities across five states, each with its own violation classification and its own methodology.

ScoreTable documents what each county does. Not what we think they should do, and not an average across all of them. What each one actually publishes.

If you''re comparing two restaurants in two counties by their published results, you''re comparing two different measurements. Worth knowing before you draw a conclusion.

The Score Behind Every Table.

ScoreTable', 'Find your county', 'Email', '[copy_state: Final] [source: ScoreTable-Email]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'ScoreTable' AND c.channel_label = 'Email' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-08-26' AND c.title = 'Why the same violation means different things in different counties');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'What a published result doesn''t tell you', 'Email', '2026-09-02', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'A published inspection result is a snapshot of one visit. It''s real information and it''s worth having. It also has limits worth understanding.

It reflects a single day. It reflects the categories that county chose to evaluate. It doesn''t reflect fire safety, which is a separate authority in every county we cover. And it doesn''t reflect what changed the day after.

None of that makes published results useless. It makes them one input rather than a verdict.

ScoreTable''s job is to make that input findable and to be honest about what it covers. One page per county across California, Oregon, Nevada, Washington, and Arizona — what''s evaluated, how, and where the official record lives.

We don''t rate. We don''t average. We don''t fill gaps with estimates.

The Score Behind Every Table.

