INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'There is no national restaurant grading system. Here''s what exists instead.', 'Blog', '2026-08-12', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'If you''ve ever seen a letter grade in a restaurant window and assumed it means the same thing everywhere, it doesn''t.

There is no national restaurant grading system in the United States. There are counties — hundreds of them — and each one decides for itself how to evaluate commercial kitchens and what, if anything, to publish.

**The four approaches**

**Letter grades.** The most visible version. Findings convert into a letter, and the establishment posts it where customers can see it. Immediate, public, and legible to anyone.

**Numeric results.** A point total published online, usually deducting from a maximum. More granular than a letter, but harder to interpret without knowing the county''s deduction methodology. Is 91 good? Depends entirely on how that county computes.

**Pass/fail or in-compliance.** Binary. No gradation between a kitchen with one minor finding and one with eleven.

**Nothing published.** Some counties inspect and document thoroughly but publish nothing to the public. The record exists — it''s simply not public-facing.

**Why the differences matter**

It isn''t only presentation. The underlying evaluation differs too.

Violation classification differs — which findings are treated as serious enough to trigger consequences. Inspection frequency differs. Re-inspection triggers differ. What gets published and where differs.

So a restaurant with a strong result in one county and a restaurant with a strong result in the next county over have satisfied two different standards. Both results are real. They''re just not the same measurement.

**What ScoreTable does**

One page per county, across California, Oregon, Nevada, Washington, and Arizona. Each page documents what that county evaluates, how its methodology works, and where the official public record lives.

**What ScoreTable deliberately doesn''t do**

We don''t rate restaurants. We don''t rank them. We don''t average county results into a comparable national scale. We don''t estimate where data is thin.

That last one matters most. The obvious product here would be a normalized national rating — take everyone''s data, convert it to one scale, publish a comparable number. It would be more useful in one narrow sense and much worse in every other.

The moment you normalize 169 different methodologies into a single scale, you''ve produced a figure no authority would recognize. And people would treat it as official, because it would look official.

We''d rather show you the real thing on its own terms, even when the real thing is messier.

**How to use this**

Find your county. Read what it evaluates and how. If you''re comparing establishments across county lines, read both counties'' methodologies before drawing conclusions.

And remember that a published result covers food safety. Fire safety — the exhaust system, the suppression system, the grease above the cooking line — is a separate authority in every county we cover, and it''s rarely part of the public record.

The Score Behind Every Table.

**ScoreTable**

*Editor note: verify each county methodology reference against the county''s published documentation before publish. Do not characterize a specific county''s practice without a primary source. No estimates, no interpolation.*', 'scoretable', 'Blog post — full', '[copy_state: Final] [source: ScoreTable-Blog]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'ScoreTable' AND c.channel_label = 'Blog' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-08-12' AND c.title = 'There is no national restaurant grading system. Here''s what exists instead.');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Two authorities decide whether a kitchen is safe. Most public data covers one.', 'Blog', '2026-08-19', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'When people look up a restaurant''s inspection record, they''re looking at food safety.

That''s genuinely useful information. It''s also one of two systems that determine whether a commercial kitchen is safe to operate in.

**The split**

Food safety — temperatures, handling, sanitation, storage, cross-contamination — is governed by your county health department, working from your state''s food code.

Fire safety in a commercial kitchen is governed by your fire authority, working primarily from NFPA 96 and related standards. That covers the exhaust hood, the ductwork, the exhaust fan, the fire suppression system, and the grease that accumulates through all of it.

These are separate authorities. Separate inspectors. Separate schedules. Separate records. In many counties, separate databases that were never designed to speak to each other.

**Why the fire side is less visible**

A few reasons, none of them sinister.

Health inspections happen on a more frequent and predictable cycle in most counties, which produces more data. Health results are more often published as part of a public transparency program. And letter-grade or numeric posting requirements are typically tied to food safety programs specifically.

Fire authority records exist. They''re simply less likely to be aggregated and published in a consumer-facing format.

**What that means for interpreting public data**

Not that public inspection data is misleading. It measures what it measures, accurately.

It means the picture is partial. A restaurant with an excellent food safety record may have an exhaust system past its service interval, and nothing in the public record would indicate that — because the public record isn''t looking at it.

**What ScoreTable does about it**

We document what each county publishes, and we document what it covers. Where a county''s public record addresses food safety only, the page says so. Where fire safety information is publicly available, we point to where it lives.

We don''t fill the gap with estimates. A gap in public data is a real thing, and describing it accurately is more useful than papering over it.

**For operators reading this**

The asymmetry cuts the other way too.

Because fire safety is less publicly visible, it''s easier to defer. Nothing posts on your door. No customer looks it up. The consequence is invisible right up until it isn''t — and then it''s the only thing anyone is discussing.

The absence of a public consequence is not the absence of a consequence.

The Score Behind Every Table.

**ScoreTable**

*Editor note: verify governing authority references before publish. Cite NFPA 96 scope precisely. Do not characterize any county''s fire records practice without a primary source.*', 'scoretable', 'Blog post — full', '[copy_state: Final] [source: ScoreTable-Blog]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'ScoreTable' AND c.channel_label = 'Blog' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-08-19' AND c.title = 'Two authorities decide whether a kitchen is safe. Most public data covers one.');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Why the same violation means different things in different counties', 'Blog', '2026-08-26', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'A temperature finding in one county might cost a small deduction. Forty miles away, the same finding might be classified as a major violation, trigger a re-inspection, and get published where every customer can see it.

Same observation. Same conditions. Different consequence.

**This isn''t inconsistent enforcement**

It''s the predictable result of 169 separate authorities across the five states we cover, each setting its own violation classification.

Each county decides which findings are serious enough to carry heavier consequences, how much a given finding affects a published result, whether repeat findings compound, and what triggers a return visit.

Those decisions are made locally, and they''re not made identically.

**Three places the difference shows up**

**Classification.** Whether a given finding is routine or major. This is the biggest single driver of consequence.

**Weighting.** In numeric counties, how many points a finding costs — and whether the same finding costs more on a repeat visit.

**Trigger thresholds.** What brings an inspector back, how quickly, and whether that return carries a fee.

**Why this breaks comparison**

If you compare two restaurants in two counties by their published results, you are comparing two different measurements.

Not two measurements of the same thing on the same scale. Two different scales, applied by two different authorities, to potentially different underlying observations.

Both results are accurate. Neither is wrong. They''re just not comparable in the way the presentation implies — and the presentation implies it strongly, because both are formatted like results.

**The trap in transparency**

More counties publish inspection data than ever, which is a genuine improvement.

It also creates something worth naming: data that looks comparable because it''s presented similarly, but isn''t, because the underlying methodologies differ.

The fix isn''t less transparency. It''s publishing methodology alongside data, so anyone reading a result knows what produced it.

**What we do**

Every ScoreTable county page carries the county''s methodology next to whatever the county publishes. Not our interpretation of it — the methodology as the county documents it, with a pointer to the source.

That''s the whole design. A number without its methodology is a number you can misread confidently.

**For operators**

If you run locations in more than one county, this is not an abstraction. The classification that applies at your first location may not apply at your second, and staff who learned the first system will apply it at the second, where it''s wrong.

Read each county''s classification separately. The comparison itself is the education.

The Score Behind Every Table.

**ScoreTable**

*Editor note: verify classification and weighting references against published county documentation. Do not name a county''s specific classification without a primary source.*', 'scoretable', 'Blog post — full', '[copy_state: Final] [source: ScoreTable-Blog]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'ScoreTable' AND c.channel_label = 'Blog' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-08-26' AND c.title = 'Why the same violation means different things in different counties');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'What a published inspection result doesn''t tell you', 'Blog', '2026-09-02', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'A published restaurant inspection result is real information. It''s worth having, and making it findable is the entire reason ScoreTable exists.

It also has limits, and understanding them makes the information more useful rather than less.

**It''s one day**

An inspection captures conditions during a specific visit. A kitchen that performed well on a Tuesday morning in March is a kitchen that performed well on a Tuesday morning in March.

That''s genuine evidence — inspectors are trained to distinguish systemic practice from a single tidy morning, and repeated findings across visits reveal patterns. But a single result is a snapshot, and snapshots have edges.

**It covers what that county evaluates**

Every county defines its own scope and classification. A result reflects the categories that county chose, weighted the way that county weights them.

Which means a result is not a general safety verdict. It''s a specific measurement against a specific local standard.

**It''s food safety, not fire safety**

In every county we cover, fire safety in a commercial kitchen is a separate authority under a separate standard. Public inspection data typically addresses food safety.

So a published result, however strong, says nothing about the exhaust system, the suppression system, or grease accumulation. Those are real risks, evaluated by someone else, usually not published in a consumer-facing format.

**It reflects conditions at the time, not after**

Things change. Staff turn over. Equipment ages. A result from eight months ago describes a kitchen that may have changed considerably.

Recency matters, and it''s worth checking the date on any result you''re reading.

**What it''s good for**

Given all that — a published result is still one of the better signals available to the public.

Patterns across multiple inspections are meaningfully informative. Major findings are meaningfully informative. A history of prompt correction is meaningfully informative.

The right frame is input, not verdict. Read the result, read the date, read the methodology that produced it, and understand what it doesn''t cover.

**Why we say all this out loud**

Because the alternative is a product that overstates what it knows.

It would be easy to present county data as a definitive safety rating. It would perform better. It would also be a claim the underlying data can''t support — and people would rely on it more heavily than it deserves precisely because it looked authoritative.

We''d rather be accurate about the limits and let people weigh it themselves.

The Score Behind Every Table.

**ScoreTable**

*Editor note: no estimates, no interpolation, no characterization of a specific county''s practice without a primary source.*', 'scoretable', 'Blog post — full', '[copy_state: Final] [source: ScoreTable-Blog]' WHERE NOT EXISTS (SELECT 1 FROM public.content_schedule c WHERE c.brand IS NOT DISTINCT FROM 'ScoreTable' AND c.channel_label = 'Blog' AND c.scheduled_date = '2026-09-02' AND c.title = 'What a published inspection result doesn''t tell you');
INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'What a published inspection record proves, precisely', 'Blog', '2026-09-09', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'A published restaurant inspection result proves something real. It''s worth being exact about what.

**What it proves**

That a specific authority evaluated a specific establishment on a specific date, against the categories that authority chose to evaluate, and recorded the findings.

That''s a verifiable fact produced by a public body with a statutory role. It isn''t opinion, it isn''t crowdsourced, and it isn''t marketing. In a landscape full of restaurant information that''s none of those things, that matters.

**What it doesn''t prove**

**Conditions today.** An inspection captures a moment. A record from six months ago describes a kitchen that has since had staff turnover, equipment aging, and possibly a change in management. The evidence is about the past; readers routinely treat it as a statement about the present.

**Fire safety.** In every county we cover, fire safety in a commercial kitchen is a separate authority operating under a separate standard. The exhaust system, the suppression system, and grease accumulation are outside the scope of a health inspection. A strong published result says nothing about any of them.

**Anything about unevaluated categories.** Every authority defines its own scope. A result reflects the categories that county chose, weighted the way it weights them. Categories outside that scope aren''t addressed — not because they''re fine, but because nobody looked.

**Consistency between visits.** A single result is a single observation. Patterns across multiple inspections carry considerably more information than any one of them, which is why we present history where a county makes it available.

**Why we''re specific about this**

The alternative is a product that overstates what it knows.

It would be easy — and commercially better — to present county inspection data as a general safety verdict. Aggregate it, brand it, publish a confident rating. People would use it. It would look authoritative, which is precisely the problem: it would be relied on more heavily than the underlying data supports.

We''d rather publish a narrow claim that''s true than a broad one that isn''t.

**Design decisions that follow from this**

**The date sits at the same visual weight as the result.** A result without its date is a claim about the present made from evidence about the past. That''s a large misreading prevented by a small design choice.

**The methodology sits next to the data.** A number without its methodology is a number you can misread confidently — and confidently is the dangerous part.

**Where a county publishes nothing, the page says so.** We don''t estimate, interpolate, or fill.

**Fire safety is named as out of scope.** Not buried in a disclaimer. Stated, because it''s the largest gap in what any public record covers.

**For operators**

There''s a version of this that cuts toward you.

Because fire safety carries no public consequence in most counties, it''s easier to defer. Nothing posts on your door. No customer looks it up. The consequence is invisible right up until it isn''t.

The absence of a public record is not the absence of a risk. It just means nobody is watching that one.

The Score Behind Every Table.

**ScoreTable**

