INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'There is no national restaurant grading system (6-8 min)', 'YouTube', '2026-08-11', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'TITLE: There Is No National Restaurant Grading System — Here''s What Exists Instead
UPLOAD: Tuesday · THUMBNAIL: four different result formats side by side, "SAME COUNTRY?"

── SCRIPT ──

[COLD OPEN]
If you''ve seen a letter grade in a restaurant window and assumed it means the same thing everywhere — it doesn''t.

There is no national restaurant grading system in the United States.

[TITLE — ScoreTable]

[VO]
There are counties. Hundreds of them. And each one decides for itself how to evaluate commercial kitchens and what, if anything, to publish.

Four approaches.

[GRAPHIC 1] Letter grades. Findings convert to a letter, posted where customers see it. Immediate, public, legible to anyone.

[GRAPHIC 2] Numeric results. A point total published online. More granular — but harder to read without knowing the county''s deduction methodology. Is 91 good? Depends entirely on how that county computes.

[GRAPHIC 3] Pass/fail. Binary. No gradation between one minor finding and eleven.

[GRAPHIC 4] And nothing published. Some counties inspect thoroughly and publish nothing publicly. The record exists — it just isn''t public-facing.

[VO — the deeper point]
And it isn''t only presentation.

Violation classification differs — which findings are serious enough to carry consequences. Inspection frequency differs. Re-inspection triggers differ.

So a strong result in one county and a strong result in the next county over have satisfied two different standards. Both real. Not the same measurement.

[VO — what we do]
ScoreTable has one page per county across California, Oregon, Nevada, Washington, and Arizona. What that county evaluates, how its methodology works, where the official record lives.

[VO — what we don''t]
And here''s what we deliberately don''t do.

We don''t rate restaurants. We don''t rank them. We don''t average county results into a national scale. We don''t estimate where data is thin.

That last one matters most.

The obvious product here would be a normalized national rating. Take everyone''s data, convert to one scale, publish a comparable number.

We won''t — because the moment you normalize 169 methodologies into one scale, you''ve produced a figure no authority would recognize.

And people would treat it as official. Because it would look official.

[CLOSE]
So: find your county. Read what it evaluates and how.

And remember a published result covers food safety. Fire safety — the exhaust system, the suppression system, the grease above the cooking line — is a separate authority in every county we cover, and it''s rarely in the public record.

The Score Behind Every Table.

[END CARD] ScoreTable

