INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Two authorities decide if a kitchen is safe (6-8 min)', 'YouTube', '2026-08-18', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'TITLE: Two Authorities Decide If A Kitchen Is Safe. Public Data Covers One.
UPLOAD: Tuesday · THUMBNAIL: two seals, gold divider, "ONLY ONE IS PUBLIC"

── SCRIPT ──

[COLD OPEN]
When you look up a restaurant''s inspection record, you''re looking at food safety.

That''s one of two systems that decide whether that kitchen is safe.

[TITLE]

[VO — the split]
Food safety — temperatures, handling, sanitation, storage, cross-contamination — is governed by your county health department, working from your state''s food code.

Fire safety in a commercial kitchen is governed by your fire authority, working primarily from NFPA 96.

That covers the exhaust hood. The ductwork. The exhaust fan. The suppression system. And the grease that accumulates through all of it.

[VO]
Separate authorities. Separate inspectors. Separate schedules. Separate records.

In a lot of counties, separate databases that were never designed to talk to each other.

[VO — why fire is less visible]
Why is the fire side less visible? A few reasons, none of them sinister.

Health inspections happen more frequently and more predictably in most counties, which produces more data.

Health results are more often part of a formal public transparency program.

And letter-grade or numeric posting requirements are usually tied to food safety programs specifically.

Fire authority records exist. They''re just less likely to be aggregated into a consumer-facing format.

[VO — what it means]
This doesn''t make public inspection data misleading. It measures what it measures, accurately.

It means the picture is partial.

A restaurant with an excellent food safety record could have an exhaust system well past its service interval — and nothing in the public record would indicate it. Because the public record isn''t looking there.

[VO — for operators]
And if you run a kitchen, the asymmetry cuts the other way.

Because fire safety is less publicly visible, it''s easier to defer. Nothing posts on your door. No customer looks it up.

The consequence is invisible right up until it isn''t. And then it''s the only thing anyone is discussing.

The absence of a public consequence is not the absence of a consequence.

[CLOSE]
ScoreTable documents what each county publishes and what it covers. Where a county''s public record addresses food safety only, our page says so.

We don''t fill the gap with estimates. A gap in public data is a real thing, and describing it accurately is more useful than papering over it.

The Score Behind Every Table.

[END CARD] ScoreTable

