INSERT INTO public.content_schedule (title, channel_label, scheduled_date, status, brand, body, cta, post_type, notes) SELECT 'Same violation, different county, different consequence (6-8 min)', 'YouTube', '2026-08-25', 'drafted', 'ScoreTable', 'TITLE: The Same Violation Means Different Things In Different Counties
UPLOAD: Tuesday · THUMBNAIL: identical finding icon, two very different outcome badges

── SCRIPT ──

[COLD OPEN]
A temperature finding in one county might cost a small deduction.

Forty miles away, the same finding might be classified as major, trigger a re-inspection, and get published where every customer can see it.

Same observation. Different consequence.

[TITLE]

[VO]
This isn''t inconsistent enforcement. It''s the predictable result of 169 separate authorities across five states, each setting its own violation classification.

Each county decides which findings are serious. How much a finding affects a published result. Whether repeat findings compound. What triggers a return visit.

Those decisions are made locally, and they''re not made identically.

[GRAPHIC — three drivers]
Three places the difference shows up.

Classification — whether a finding is routine or major. Biggest single driver of consequence.

Weighting — in numeric counties, how many points it costs, and whether it costs more on a repeat.

Trigger thresholds — what brings an inspector back, how fast, and whether that return carries a fee.

[VO — why comparison breaks]
Which means if you compare two restaurants in two counties by their published results, you''re comparing two different measurements.

Not two measurements of the same thing on the same scale. Two different scales, applied by two different authorities.

Both accurate. Neither wrong. Just not comparable in the way the presentation implies.

And the presentation implies it strongly — because both are formatted like results.

[VO — the trap]
More counties publish inspection data than ever. That''s a real improvement.

It also creates something worth naming: data that looks comparable because it''s formatted similarly, but isn''t, because the methodologies differ.

The fix isn''t less transparency. It''s publishing methodology alongside data.

[VO — what we do]
Every ScoreTable county page carries the county''s methodology next to whatever the county publishes. Not our interpretation — the methodology as the county documents it, with a pointer to the source.

That''s the whole design.

A number without its methodology is a number you can misread confidently.

[CLOSE — for operators]
And if you run locations in more than one county, this isn''t abstract.

The classification at your first location may not apply at your second. Staff who learned the first system will apply it at the second, where it''s wrong.

Read each county''s classification separately. The comparison itself is the education.

The Score Behind Every Table.

[END CARD] ScoreTable

